<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Custody ~ what a vessel keeps at rest, and what one face carries away

A vessel keeps private files: keys, rosters, names, and the state its key shore needs. This carrier states the law of those files at rest. It names the one table that lists them, the bundle that carries one face off the device, and the act that brings a face back on a fresh vessel.

> ''Register.'' The operator approved the bundle's model as presented, the same-face continuity ruling and the statement of reach among its points (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>). The house settled the bytes, the row dispositions, the refusal names and each reading marked below under that approval.

''Words.'' The ''vessel key'' seals a vessel's private files (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/keys">>). A ''route'' names what a person holds to open a key: a passphrase, or a paper sheet. A ''seat'' names one vessel's own place in a face's key group. A ''content key'' opens the sealed values of one bag at one key version. A ''face's root'' names the persona root seed a face signs its lineage with. The ''serving key'' (SK) seals the serving rows alone, and the vessel key and each ember slot wrap it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/the-serving-key">>). The ''outbox key'' seals the sealed store's outbox parcels, and the vessel key wraps it (#/the-table, the outbox).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-table>>

!! The carrier table ~ one list, every reader derives

''One table names every private file a vessel keeps at rest'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts`). Each row states the file's home, its custody class, its secret kind, its writer, whose it reads as, and how a bundle treats it. No second list of file names stands anywhere: a file a second list omits would stay unsealed while a sweep reports success, and a file a second list invents would get written over by an act nobody owns.

!!! Five custody classes

|! class |! what holds it |! who reads it |
| `floor` | nothing: it rests outside every key | the floor, before any key opens: the vessel's own key, its inception, the slot tree that wraps the vessel key and the serving tree that wraps SK |
| `floor-plain` | nothing of the vessel's: plain bytes, or bytes sealed under a key the vessel key never wraps | the floor's own doors, or anyone: bytes public by construction, or bytes a blind carrier may hold |
| `serving` | SK, which the vessel key and each ember slot wrap | the vessel at SERVING, once its embers answer a raise, and the unlocked vessel, through the vessel key's entry in the serving tree |
| `hot` | the vessel key | the vessel, once at unlock, for the life of that unlock |
| `cold` | the vessel key | one act, which opens the row, uses it and drops it |

A private file the floor never reads rides `hot`: the seal costs it nothing, and it closes the social graph at rest.

''The `serving` class holds the serving rows and nothing beside them'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>). SK seals exactly the rows a serving gate and blind carriage read and write for parties already standing (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/the-tiers">>), so a holder of the disk who gathers the embers' answers opens those rows and no `hot` or `cold` row. ''No serving row holds a minting secret'': no epoch secret, hosting key, leaf seed or signing key rests under SK, and a walker's grant checks at SERVING under a public half alone, a shape the hosted road's re-cut lands before the ember's walker arm. No serving row opens under the vessel key, and no vessel-key row opens under SK. A vessel-key rotation re-wraps the vessel key's entry in the serving tree and re-seals no serving row; an SK rotation re-seals every serving row and touches no `hot` or `cold` row. When an ember slot answers with no person's confirm, and what a hearth with no hardware anchor asks instead, reads in the ember's own law (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/the-raise">>).

!!! What the table decides

* ''The disk names the carriers.'' A carrier reads as a file that exists. No writer keeps a roster of its own files, and the census reads the directories the rows name. A stray file spelled like a row becomes that row: the census errs toward governing more, which refuses a write, and never toward governing less, which destroys a secret.
* ''Rows span three homes'': the identity home, the storage home and the seal home. The census walks only the subtrees some row names.
* ''The rotation reads the rows.'' Every `hot` and `cold` file standing counts as sealed under the vessel key, and a rotation re-seals exactly that list. Every `serving` file counts as sealed under SK, and an SK rotation re-seals exactly that list. A sealed file no row names refuses either rotation, so none stays behind under a retired key.
* ''An unbind that rotates runs as ONE act.'' `lares vault unbind <slot-id> --rotate` reads each staying slot's route again, then hands those slots to the rotation. The rotation's census and its opening of every carrier pass before any byte moves, and its one commit lands a tree that holds the staying slots under a new vessel key. A carrier that refuses the rotation therefore leaves the removed slot bound, and no tree ever stands between the two.
* ''A row outside every key names its law.'' Every `floor` and `floor-plain` row states, in the table's own column, why its bytes rest bare (#/the-table, the rows that rest bare). A `serving`, `hot` or `cold` row states none, so a `serving`, `hot` or `cold` file that reads anything but sealed reads as a FAULT.
* ''A file spelled like a row answers to that row's law.'' A row never excuses the file it matched. A `floor` or `floor-plain` file that rests under the vessel key's seal or under SK's reads as a FAULT, since no rotation re-seals it. A row that reads its law off the bytes (the sealed store's rests) faults a file whose name fails to commit to its bytes, and a file whose bytes fail to read whole as the kind its shelf names, so a clear key under a rest's spelling reads as a FAULT. An outbox parcel that fails to read as a sealed parcel reads as a FAULT (#/the-table, the outbox). ''The census reads a vessel's own writers, and it catches no planted secret under a row's exact spelling where the row reads no law off the bytes.'' A note of the sealed store, a Keyhive chunk, a contact card, a charter, a consent and an admit pin carry no check of their bytes, so a clear key a hand drops under one of those spellings reads as that row, bare and with no fault, and stays off the list of files outside the table. Whoever writes such a file already holds the disk.
* ''The census names the private files outside the table, within the three homes.'' One reader walks each home whole, the subtrees no row names among them, and names every file there that reads private and answers to no row: a file whose mode admits its owner alone, a file under the vessel key's seal or SK's, and a sidecar a writer left standing. `lares vault status` prints that list beside ONE reading of each standing file, and a witness refuses a founded vessel that holds one. ''Its reach ends at the three homes'': the config home, the projected files and a store a setting moved elsewhere stand outside it. Its mode sign reads what a writer chose: a file whose mode admits a group reads as no private file, a link reads as no file, and under a umask that strips group and other from every file the reading names files no writer marked. It errs toward naming more.
* ''A row names whose it reads as'': one face's own (its handle-index), or the vessel's.
* ''The slot tree wraps a key under human routes'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/keyslot.ts`; the operator's rulings stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>). Each slot wraps the key under a slot key, and splits that slot key across its pins: a passphrase, stretched by scrypt, or a paper sheet that carries 32 random bytes. Any one slot opens the key, and a tree always keeps one route. The tree commits to the one key it wraps, so a slot spliced in from another tree opens nothing. It rests as plain bytes: a random id for each slot, a threshold, each pin's kind, salts, nonces and wrapped bytes, and no version, counter or clock.
* ''A row names how a bundle treats it'': `whole` (the row rides as it rests), `lines` (the one face's own lines ride, and nothing of another face), or `stays` (the row never leaves its vessel). A bundle's cut and a restore's check both derive from this column, so no hand picks the rows a bundle carries.

!!! How a bundle treats each row

|! row |! bundle |! why |
| `persona-root` | `whole` | the face's root: its lineage |
| `face-proof-key` | `whole` | the face's own key at one Nexus, held by the root-holding vessel; while a re-issue stands half done the row holds the standing key and its successor, and a bundle refuses to cut until the re-issue lands |
| `anchors` | `lines` | the face's fixed prefix and its signer's name ride; the veil tag, the device edge and the key group's ids name one vessel's seat, and stay |
| `persona-roster` | `lines` | the one line that names this face's root |
| `persona-petnames` | `lines` | this face's own pet-name |
| `persona-declarations` | `lines` | this face's declared Handle; the seat claim and the chair label name a seat on one node, and stay |
| `handle-book` | `lines` | the records this face recognised, each with this face's own label. A record that names no recognising face stays on its vessel and enters no bundle (house reading) |
| `veil-archive`, `keyhive-archive` | `stays` | a seat's own key state: a carried copy would let its holder sign as that seat |
| `seat-key` | `stays` | a chair's own key in a charter: a carried copy would let its holder sign as that chair (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>) |
| `restore-pending` | `stays` | a restore's own commit row: it holds what one restore still owes this vessel, and leaves once that restore lands (#/the-restore) |
| `sk-slots`, `sk-rotation-owed`, every `serving` row | `stays` | the serving tier belongs to its hearth: a restored vessel mints its own SK and binds fresh embers (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/meets">>) |
| `vessel-face-key`, `gate-memory`, `device-share`, `public-handles`, every other row | `stays` | each belongs to its vessel, or names another face, or rebuilds from a board |

!!! The rows that rest bare, each by law

|! row |! class |! why it rests outside the vessel key |
| `vessel-key` | `floor` | the floor signs with it before any person unlocks, so no vessel key may stand between a boot and it |
| `vessel-kel` | `floor` | the vessel key's own inception names that key and carries its signature: it stands public and proves itself, and a load of the key reads it before any person unlocks |
| `vk-slots` | `floor` | the slot tree wraps the vessel key under each human route, so the key it wraps cannot seal it |
| `sk-slots` | `floor` | the serving tree wraps SK under the vessel key's entry and each ember slot, so the key it wraps cannot seal it, and a raise reads it before any key opens (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/the-serving-key">>) |
| `sk-rotation-owed` | `floor` | a deny-only mark in the serving tree: any counted removal sets it, and while it stands the serving tree refuses every ask, until the SK rotation at a human unlock clears it. It holds no secret, and a raise must read it with no key open |
| `vessel-card` | `floor-plain` | a contact card holds public keys alone, and the vessel hands it to every peer it meets |
| `charter`, `carried-charter` | `floor-plain` | the floor's gate reads each charter before any key opens, and a charter stands public and signed |
| `carriage-consent`, `carriage-admit` | `floor-plain` | the floor reads a kept consent and an admit pin before any key opens; each names a charter, a reach and a gate's public key, and holds no secret |
| `nexus-key-line` | `floor-plain` | a key hand-over names two public keys and both their signatures, and it stands public on the Nexus's carriage board |
| `sealed-rest`, `sealed-chunk` | `floor-plain` | a lockbox, a record, an index row and a Keyhive chunk hold ciphertext, opaque ids and addresses, and no secret, name or key in the clear; a blind carrier may hold every one (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-store">>) |
| `sealed-own` | `floor-plain` | the vessel's own note for its sealed store holds opaque ids, a one-byte mark or nothing, under an opaque name |

Every `serving` row rests sealed under SK. Every other row rests `hot` or `cold`, sealed under the vessel key, save the outbox's parcels, which rest sealed under the outbox key the vessel key wraps (below).

''The `sk-rotation-owed` mark binds an honest disk alone'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation). A hand that writes an older serving tree onto the disk restores the removed pin and omits the mark, and that hand already holds the disk; no fence here detects the rollback (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/a-thief">>).

!!! The outbox ~ sealed parcels, random names, and a crash law

''A locked disk names no reader'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>). A parcel carries Keyhive operations that name the readers a vessel wrapped a key for, so its row leaves `floor-plain` for a sealed class.

* ''One outbox key, stable, wrapped by the vessel key.'' The vessel key wraps the outbox key, and an unlock hands it to the daemon island the way content keys reach that island. The bare outbox key rests nowhere beside the parcels: a locked vessel holds it wrapped alone, and a vessel-key rotation re-wraps it and re-seals no parcel.
* ''Each parcel seals whole'' under randomized authenticated encryption keyed from the outbox key. The parcel's reader and its routing ride inside the sealed body.
* ''A parcel's name carries no reader digest.'' Each parcel rests under a random name, so a holder of the disk counts parcels and weighs each, and reads no reader.
* ''The crash law.'' A parcel commits by one atomic write of its whole sealed file. Before that commit nothing stands sendable. After it, and before the member lane ships the parcel, the parcel opens again at the next unlock and the lane retries it. After the ship and before the local delete, the lane may send the parcel again, and the receiver folds the repeat idempotently.
* ''The island writes parcels only while unlocked'', since it holds the outbox key for the life of an unlock alone.

''Which sealed class the row names'' (`hot` beside an outbox-key row, or a class of its own) rides the build that seals the outbox, under this law (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-loops">>).

!!! What a row's name says at rest

> ''House reading.'' The seal covers a row's bytes and never its name. This section states the residue of the names plainly, as #/the-bundle states the residue of a bundle.

* ''A holder of the disk with no route counts the rows.'' The file names, their count and each file's length rest in the clear in all three homes.
* ''The carriage rows enumerate.'' A hearth's carriage for a walker rests one row per carried value, and each row's name carries the walker's own key segment, the value's address and its length. A holder of the disk therefore lists each walker's rows and weighs each walker's bytes, and opens none of them.
* ''A name cut from a digest answers a test and no listing.'' A hosting home names itself by a digest of its Nexus's identifier, a walk row by a digest of its hearth's gate key, a spend row by a digest of its nonce or its marker, a face's per-Nexus key row by the face's index and a digest of its Nexus, a chair's key row by a digest of that key's public half, and a gate's memory row by its face's proof-subject digest. A holder of the disk who ALREADY holds one of those values tests whether its row stands: whether this vessel hosts that Nexus, walks at that hearth, burnt that leaf, or holds that chair's key. A holder who lacks the value recovers none of them from a name. A chair's key row takes its name from the key alone, with no Nexus in the digest, so one key gives one name on every disk that holds it: a chair's key mints at random for one chair on one vessel, and a second disk that shows the same name holds a copy of that row.
* ''The plain rows name each carried Nexus.'' A carried charter, a kept consent and an admit pin rest in the clear by law, so a holder of the disk reads which Nexuses this vessel carries and where each gate answers.
* ''The sealed store's rests link, and its outbox names no reader.'' A lockbox's name leads with its key version and its bag's locator, and an index row's name and bytes carry the locator, the key version, the entry's locator, its role and its parents' addresses: opaque ids, which a holder of the disk counts and links to one another. A parcel in the outbox rests sealed under a random name, so a holder of the disk counts the parcels, weighs each and sees when each arrives and leaves, and reads no reader.
* ''The serving tree names its pins and its mark.'' `sk-slots` rests bare, so a holder of the disk counts the ember slots, reads each threshold, and reads whether `sk-rotation-owed` stands.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-bundle>>

!! The bundle ~ one face, sealed under a route a person holds

''A bundle holds ONE face.'' It rests sealed under a route the person holds, a passphrase or a paper sheet, and never under a vessel key. A lost device takes its vessel key with it; the bundle opens without one.

!!! The bytes

| part | holds |
| the format mark | the first eight bytes of SHA-256 over the format's name. A changed format takes a new name and so a new mark; the mark holds no counter |
| the slot tree | the bundle key, wrapped under each route the person bound, in the same shape that wraps a vessel key (#/the-table, the slot tree) |
| the sealed body | everything else, under authenticated encryption keyed from the bundle key |

* ''One fresh bundle key per cut.'' The house draws 32 random bytes for each bundle and uses them once.
* ''Authenticated encryption covers the whole inner body.'' A reader recomputes the tag before it reads one field, and a body altered by one bit opens for nobody.
* ''Nothing rests outside the seal but the format mark and the slot tree.'' Two bundles share no key, no salt, no identifier and no visible field.
* ''The residue, stated plainly.'' A holder of the file with no route reads its length, the count of slots, each slot's kind (passphrase or paper) and each slot's threshold. The length grows with the count of carried key versions, proof-key rows and handle-book records.

!!! The standing section ~ lineage

The body carries the face's root and the lines that let the face stand again: the root row, the roster line that names it, the anchors' prefix and signer name, each of the face's `face-proof-key` rows, its pet-name, its declared Handle, and the handle-book records that face itself recognised.

''The face's key history rides beside them.'' The body carries the face's key event log as the cutting vessel's board held it: public events, each one verifiable by itself, from the inception to the head. A restore therefore always reads a key history, on a vessel that reached no board too. The log adds no authority: every event in it already stood on a board.

''What a bundle never carries.'' No veil key and no veil Archive of any vessel. No vessel key. No `vessel-face-key`. No gate memory. No seat claim, no chair label and no chair's key. No row of another face. No keyring. A bundle therefore carries LESS authority than the vessel it left: it holds no key that signs as any seat that ever stood.

!!! The memory section ~ content keys, never a seat key

''Memory rides as content keys.'' The section carries the bare content keys the cutting vessel's holder COUNTED AND HELD for this face at the cut, one per bag and key version. Each entry names its bag, the bag's locator, the key version id and the key. The key version id commits the key and the bag (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-records">>).

''The section seals inside the key island, under the bundle key.'' The bundle key alone derives one key pair for the section. The daemon island, where the content-key holder lives, seals the section to that pair's public half and answers ciphertext, so the bundle key's holder alone opens it and no secret crosses the island's port at the cut. An import derives the pair's secret half from the bundle key and hands it to the taking vessel's island sealed to a key that island named for that one import; the island opens the section inside itself. A bare content key never crosses a port in the clear, at the cut or at the import (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-store">>).

!!! What a bundle reaches, stated without softening

* ''A bundle opens every surviving value EVER sealed under a version it carries, INCLUDING values written after the cut under that same version.'' A writer keeps sealing under a head until a roll; the old bundle's key opens each of those values.
* ''It opens no version minted after the cut.'' Those versions reach the restored seat as they reach any newly admitted seat, by the roll and hand-over law. A person cuts a fresh bundle after a roll to cover the newer span.
* ''The house says at the cut which bags and versions the bundle holds.''
* ''Memory needs BOTH halves, and the bundle holds one.'' The keys ride the bundle. The sealed BYTES must survive elsewhere: a carrier that held them blind, another seat, or a copy of the vessel's `sealed/` and `cad/` rests. The house says so at the cut and again at the restore.
* ''No death rite.'' After an import the vessel holds those keys as an ordinary seat holds them. An old bundle keeps opening exactly what it carried. A removal protects later versions alone: a removed reader keeps what it read, and its bundle adds durability and a theft target, never a new entitlement.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-cut>>

!! The cut ~ `lares vault backup --face <N>`

# The person names a face this vessel holds a root for, and a file to write.
# The command reads two things on a terminal, by the custody prompt's law: no flag, no environment variable and no pipe carries either. First the vessel's own route, which proves a person stands at this cut. Then the bundle's route: a new passphrase typed twice, or a new paper sheet read back word for word. A harness cuts through the test door, which acts on a marked scratch root alone.
# The command draws the bundle key and binds the slot tree in its own process.
# The standing vessel builds the body behind its custody door: it reads the standing section off its sealed rows, and it asks its daemon island for the memory section.
# The command writes the format mark, the slot tree and the sealed body to the file, then prints which bags and versions the bundle holds, and that the sealed bytes must survive elsewhere.

''The cut needs the standing vessel, unlocked, wearing that face.'' The content-key holder lives in the standing vessel's daemon island and counts keys for the face that vessel wears. A cut of another face refuses by name; the person wears that face, stands the vessel again, and cuts.

''The cut asks the vessel's own route again'' (house reading). An unlocked vessel's custody door answers any process of its own user, and no act of its table hands out a root. A bundle rebuilds a root for whoever holds its route, so the cut rides a frame of its own that carries the vessel's route. A route that opens no slot spends one of the boot's unlock attempts.

''The cut refuses under the vessel's own edge shadow'' (`own-edge-shadowed`). A cut hands keys beyond the holder, and a vessel under its own shadow performs no key act (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys">>). ''The cut reads the shadow once, at its entry.'' A raise the vessel counts while a cut runs binds its next act: the bundle that cut produced stands, and the next cut refuses. The vessel's main thread takes that one reading before any row opens, and says so inside the ask it signs for the memory section; the island's holder then reads none of its own.

!!! The cut's refusals

|! refusal |! what stopped it |
| `custody-locked` | the vessel holds no key |
| `no-slot-opens` · `attempts-spent` | the vessel's own route opened no slot, or the boot's attempts stand spent |
| `face-not-held` | this vessel holds no root at that handle-index |
| `face-not-worn` | the vessel wears another face, so its holder counts no key for this one |
| `own-edge-shadowed` | the vessel reads a shadow over its own edge |
| `key-history-unread` | this vessel's board holds no key history for the face that verifies whole from its inception |
| `memory-unavailable` | no content-key holder stands in this vessel's island, or the holder refused the section |
| `out-exists` | a file already stands at the path the person named |

A cut that refuses writes no file, and a cut never writes over one: a standing file refuses before any frame leaves the command.

''The vessel checks in a fixed order.'' The custody door reads the frame's shape, then the lock, then the route. The cut then reads its own shadow and refuses under it before any row opens. It reads the roster, the worn face, each row the table's column names, the key history, and last the island's memory section. A refusal at any step answers its name alone.

''A vessel whose holder counts no key cuts a bundle with an empty memory section.'' The section still rides, sealed to the pair the bundle key derives, and the command says that it holds no content key.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-restore>>

!! The restore ~ a recovery act, never a row copy

''`lares vault restore <file>` brings a face back onto a hearth that never held it.'' The fresh hearth derives its OWN veil from its own custody root and takes a NEW seat, and the restored face's device edge binds the hearth that vessel stands at: its own seed charter, or the hearth an admit joined it to. No seat of the lost vessel stands again. The face lands at the handle-index its bundle named, or at the index a person names (`--as <N>`); on a fresh hearth persona zero holds index zero, so the restored face lands beside it.

!!! The steps

# ''Open.'' The command reads the bundle's route on a terminal, opens the slot tree and hands the body and the bundle key to the vessel's custody door. The body's tag verifies before any field reads.
# ''Preflight, before any write.'' The vessel reads every target row and every relation, the names rows and the handle-book among them: a row that stands and does not read whole refuses here, by its own name. The check of the board reads and never writes: a board this vessel never held reads as holding no event. A founded place stands here, the hearth the restored face binds stands (its seed charter, or the hearth an admit joined it to), and a face above index zero rides beside a lit founding face. The root's public key derives from its seed, the roster line names that key, and the prefix and signer name match what the root itself derives. The carried key history verifies whole, from the inception that root derives. ''The face's CURRENT key history still accepts the root'': every key event this vessel reads for the face, off the carried log and off its own board, folds through the one reader of key histories, and the newest event of that lineage still commits recovery to this root's own recovery key and still names this root as the operating key. A rotation still open to contest counts as the newest event. A history that forks refuses: no reader settles a fork by order. An event that fails its own proof moves nothing, so nobody blocks a restore by planting one. No root of this face stands here. The target handle-index holds no other root, no other roster line and no lit face; an orphan root row there matches the bundle's key. No `face-proof-key` row stands there under other bytes.
# ''One durable commit.'' The vessel seals ONE row, `restore-pending`, that holds everything the restore will land. That one write commits the restore: before it nothing moved, and after it the restore completes, in this run or the next. The vessel then lands each row from it: the proof-key rows, the names, the handle-book records, the root row and its roster line. Each lands only where nothing stands or where the same bytes stand, so a run that stops anywhere lands the rest when the same command runs again.
# ''Re-admit.'' The vessel seats the carried key history on its own board and lights the face under its own veil: a fresh key group, the same fixed prefix, and a device edge the root signs for this vessel's key. The pending row leaves once the face stands lit. The face mounts when the vessel stands again and a person unlocks it.
# ''Import'' (`lares vault restore <file> --memory`). The vessel, standing unlocked and wearing the face, hands the memory section to its daemon island's import door (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-store">>). The command prints each version's outcome, and that the sealed bytes must stand in this vessel's rests for a body to open.

''A stopped restore completes.'' A pending row that names the same root resumes at the landing, from the row and never from the bundle handed in. The key history check runs again first, over the row's log, the handed bundle's log and the board, so another cut of the face that carries a newer head refuses the resume. A pending row that names another face refuses, and so does another index named over a restore that stands pending.

''Restore lands additively.'' It never replaces a newer lockbox, record, key, name or label with a bundle's snapshot. A name fills only where the vessel holds none. An old bundle over a newer holder adds what the holder lacks and moves nothing it holds.

''A vessel checks the newest key history it reads, and every honest reader checks its own'' (house reading). The house keeps no registry of heads, and no vessel reads a head it never received. The restore answers which head its check read, by sequence, and whether it read that head off this vessel's own board or off the bundle's log. The carried log reaches as far as the cut. A rotation that landed after the cut shows only on a board, so a fresh vessel that reached no board passes a root the face has since superseded. That vessel's seat then stands for itself alone: every reader that holds the newer head refuses the edge the superseded root signed, at its own gate, and this vessel's own gate refuses it too once its board carries that head.

''A face whose other vessels still stand takes the join door'' (house reading). A restore founds a fresh key group under the face's fixed prefix. Where another vessel of the face still holds the group, that vessel seats the new device through the fleet-join door, and the bundle serves that device's memory alone (`--memory`).

!!! The provisional standing ~ a restored face waits on one party to its relation

''A restored face stands PROVISIONAL'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>). The restore read the newest key history this vessel holds, and no vessel reads a head it never received, so the face waits until at least one party to its relation shows its own log view of the face and that view agrees with the head the restore read.

* ''Who confirms'': a sibling device of the same human, a seated chair of a Nexus the face stands in, or one of the face's guardians. Each shows the log view it holds itself; the house keeps no registry of heads, and a confirm reads no list compiled about the face.
* ''What the face does while provisional'': it reads, recovers and serves its own content. It signs no act that changes who holds authority, and each such act refuses by name and names who could confirm.
* ''What the restore says'': which head it read and from where (this vessel's board, or the bundle's log), and, while the face stays provisional, the parties that could confirm it.
* ''Where every such party stays unreachable'', the guardians' recovery path answers, under the impersonation quorum, and never the local read alone.
* ''The residue, stated plainly'': one agreeing party may itself sit stale, so its agreement counts as evidence that no fork shows to that party, never as proof that no unseen head stands.

!!! The restore's refusals

|! refusal |! what stopped it |
| `bundle-unreadable` | no file reads at the path the person named |
| `bundle-malformed` | the file carries no format mark, or no slot tree that reads whole |
| `no-slot-opens` | the route opened no slot of the bundle |
| `bundle-refused` | the body's tag failed: altered bytes, or another bundle's key |
| `bundle-inconsistent` | the body's root, roster line, prefix or signer name disagree with one another, or the carried key history fails to verify from that root's own inception |
| `root-superseded` | the newest head this vessel reads commits recovery to another key set, or names another operating key |
| `face-already-held` | this vessel holds this face's root and no restore of it stands pending: a second restore over it |
| `restore-pending` | another face's restore stands pending here; the same command over that face's own bundle completes it first |
| `place-unfounded` | no founded place stands here: a restore lands a face onto a vessel `lares vessel found` already stood |
| `founding-face-unlit` | the target index lies above zero, and no founding face stands lit on this vessel |
| `no-hearth` | no seed charter stands here and no admit joined this vessel to a hearth, so no hearth stands for the restored face to bind to; the person founds the hearth first, by the Stable founding act |
| `index-held` | the target handle-index holds another root or a lit face |
| `roster-names-another-key` | a roster line at the target index names another key |
| `orphan-names-another-key` | a root row stands at the target index under another key |
| `row-conflict` | a row the restore would write stands under other bytes |
| `face-not-worn` | `--memory` met a vessel that stands wearing no face |
| `another-face` | `--memory` met a bundle whose root differs from the face this vessel wears |
| `memory-unavailable` | `--memory` met a vessel with no daemon island, or no vessel standing |
| `signer-unavailable` | `--memory` met an island that holds no session signer: an import signs a record |

Every refusal of a restore lands before the commit row, so before any row moves. An import that refuses lands no key; each entry of an import that runs draws one outcome of its own (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-store">>).

''The restore's two acts ride the custody door's act table'' (house reading). The table takes no credential of the vessel, and nothing a row takes opens the vessel key. The two rows take a bundle's body still sealed and the bundle's own key, which the person's route already opened in the command's process: that key opens one bundle's body and nothing this vessel holds. A root comes in through the restore's row, and no row answers one. With no vessel standing the command runs the same restore in its own process; an import needs the standing vessel's island.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/a-thief>>

!! What a thief learns

!!! A thief of the medium ~ the file, and no route

* That the file reads as a house bundle, by its format mark.
* The residue of #/the-bundle: the length, the slot count, each slot's kind and threshold.
* No face, no key, no bag, no name, no Nexus, and no link to any other bundle: two unopened bundles share nothing.
* A passphrase route yields to guessing at the cost of one scrypt derivation per guess, offline and without limit. A paper route carries 256 random bits and yields to no guessing.

!!! A thief who opens it ~ the file and a route

* ''The face's root.'' Wherever the face's current recovery history still accepts that root, the thief restores the face onto a vessel of their own, takes a NEW seat, and signs as the face from there. That face stands provisional (#/the-restore), so it signs no act that changes who holds authority until a party to the face's relation shows a view that agrees with its head. A guardian set grafted in since, or a rotation, ends that reach for readers who hold the newer head.
* ''The face's proof key'' at each Nexus the bundle carried a row for.
* ''The face's private past under the carried versions'': every surviving value ever sealed under them, values written after the cut included, wherever the thief also obtains the sealed bytes.
* ''Names'': the pet-name, the declared Handle, the handle-book records of that face with its labels, and every bag id and key version id the memory section names.
* ''No old seat.'' The bundle holds no veil key and no vessel key, so the thief signs as no seat that ever stood.
* ''No authorship by a content key.'' A content key signs nothing: an honest reader counts a record only under a capable session signer, so a thief with keys alone authors nothing any honest reader counts. Outside the honest reader, a bare key plus ciphertext decrypts; that reads as reading, which the bundle grants by design.
* ''Linkage.'' Someone who opens two bundles of two faces that held one bag sees equal bag and version ids. Unopened, nothing links them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-the-code-holds>>

!! What the code holds

''Built: a production path reaches each.''
* ''The carrier table'', its census, the rotation's list, its bundle column and its law column (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts`; `bundleRows` names every row a bundle carries, and the row's own type refuses a `floor` or `floor-plain` row that states no law).
* ''The census of what stands outside the table'' (`vault-carriers.ts` `outsideTheTable`; `custody-session.ts` `custodyStatus`): one reading of each standing file, a fault mark on any `hot` or `cold` row that reads anything but sealed, and every private file no row names.
* ''`lares vault status`'' (`packages/lares-cli/src/commands/vault.ts`): the slot tree, that one reading of each file with its law or its fault, and each file outside the table by name. The door answers seven verbs (`status` · `unlock` · `lock` · `bind` · `unbind` · `backup` · `restore`), and a credential rides a terminal alone.
* ''The unbind that rotates'' (`custody-session.ts` `unbindCustodySlot`): one act, whose census passes before any byte moves.
* ''The custody census witness'' (`tools/custody-census-witness.sh`): it founds a vessel on a marked scratch root with the built tree, stands it, unlocks it, and holds the command's own reading to the table. It plants an owner-only file, a sidecar and a sealed file no row names, and reads each named before it trusts a clean reading; the sealed stray refuses an unbind that rotates and leaves both slots bound, and the same act completes once the stray leaves. The live cut and the live restore hold their own vessels to the same reading (`tools/custody-census.mjs`).
* ''The bundle's bytes'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-bundle.ts`): the format mark, the slot tree, the sealed body under a key the bundle key derives, the memory pair that key derives under another name, and a body reader that refuses any part the shape does not name.
* ''The cut'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-cut.ts`): one extractor for each row the table's column names, the shadow read once at the entry, and each refusal of #/the-cut. A test holds the extractors equal to the column.
* ''The custody door's `backup` frame'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/custody-frames.ts`) and the holder's route proof (`custody-holder.ts` `proves`): the frame carries the vessel's own route, a face and a bundle key, and nothing beside them.
* ''The memory section's cut'': the vessel's main thread signs one ask of its daemon island (`packages/lararium-node/src/island-verb-ask.ts` `askIslandMemory`), and the island seals the section to the pair the bundle key derives (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/what-the-code-holds">>). No content key mints on a production vessel until a session signer stands, so a production cut carries an empty section.
* ''`lares vault backup --face <N> --out <file>`'' (`packages/lares-cli/src/vault-backup.ts`, `commands/vault.ts`): the command reads the vessel's route and the bundle's new route on a terminal, binds the slot tree in its own process and writes the file owner-only. The test door for marked scratch roots (`tools/backup-test-custody.mjs`) runs the same cut under the published test route.
* ''The handle-book's tag for the recognising face'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/handle-book.ts`): a recognition made while a face stands worn tags that face's root key, `snapshotFor` answers one face's own records under that face's own labels, and a record no face tagged enters no bundle. The node command line tags; a browser vessel cuts no bundle and tags nothing.

* ''The restore'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-restore.ts`; the act table's `bundle.restore` row, `sealed-acts.ts`): the five steps of #/the-restore, each refusal by name before the one commit row, and a stopped restore that completes from that row. The face lights through the same rite that lights any face, over the root the restore landed.
* ''`lares vault restore <file>`'' (`packages/lares-cli/src/vault-restore.ts`, `commands/vault.ts`): the command reads the bundle's route on a terminal, opens the slot tree in its own process and hands the body still sealed, beside the bundle key, to one act. The test door for marked scratch roots (`tools/restore-test-custody.mjs`) runs the same restore under the published test route.
* ''The chair's key row'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/seat-key-store.ts`; the table's `seat-key` row). One cold row holds one chair's key, 32 bytes minted at random, under a name that carries the face whose seat claim stood and a digest of the key's public half. The store mints, signs one image for one opening, lists by name alone, answers one face's public halves for one read, and retires a row its own vessel key opens once that row's bytes answer the key asked for. The seal rite mints each standing chair's key there (`seal-ceremony.ts` `armGenesis`), adopts at a roll each cold key a person typed back from its card, once its digest answers the standing head (`armRoll`), signs a roll as a seat (`seal.sign`), and retires a key no held charter seats and no open crossing names (`retireSeats`); the quorum doors sign with the rows (`held-quorum.ts`). No chair's NEXT key rests on the vessel: it rests on its card.
* ''The end-to-end rehearsal'' (`packages/lararium-node/tests/face-bundle-rehearsal.test.ts`, on the real founding, the real face rite and each vessel's own key island; `tools/vault-restore-door.test.mjs`, on the built tree and standing vessels): found, light a face, seal a private body, cut, destroy the vessel's home, found fresh, restore, import, open the body and a second body sealed after the cut under the same version; a second restore and another identity refuse.

''Specified in code: the suites run it, and no production path completes it.''
* ''The memory import'' (`face-bundle-restore.ts` `importFaceBundleMemory`; the act table's `bundle.import` row). The standing vessel places the island's door and its import under its own signed asks. An import signs a record, and no production path opens a session signer, so on a production vessel it refuses `signer-unavailable`. Caller that completes it: the wire cutover's session open (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed">>).

''Owed: ruled, and no code holds it.'' Each rides a row of <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-loops">>.
* ''The `serving` class'': the carrier table's class, `sk-slots`, `sk-rotation-owed`, the serving rows under SK, the SK rotation, and the census, `vault status` and the rotation's list reading all five classes (train E, beside the ember's serving tier).
* ''The sealed outbox'': the outbox key under the vessel key and its hand-off to the daemon island at unlock, each parcel sealed whole under a random name, the crash law, and the row's sealed class (train K). Until it lands, the `sealed-outbox` row rests `floor-plain` in code (`vault-carriers.ts`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-store-rest.ts`), and a parcel's name leads with a digest of its reader.
* ''The provisional standing'': the restored face's provisional mark, the refusal of each authority-changing act while it stands, the confirm by a party's own log view, and the restore's line that names who could confirm (train K; `face-bundle-restore.ts`, `vault-restore.ts`).

<<~/ahu>>

